post_install() {
  cat <<'MSG'

  discord-ducking installed system-wide. Config is per-user, so now run:

      discord-ducking install

  Then three manual steps:

    1. Discord -> Settings -> Voice & Video -> Output Device
          select "Discord (ducking source)"

    2. discord-ducking verify

    3. discord-ducking measure        # while someone is TALKING in a call
       CALIBRATE THE THRESHOLD. It is machine-specific -- the commonly
       suggested -30 dB never triggers on some systems, where real speech
       peaks around -35 dBFS. Skipping this is the #1 cause of
       "everything looks right but nothing ducks".

  Proof it works:   discord-ducking test
  Remove:           discord-ducking uninstall   (before pacman -R)
  Docs:             /usr/share/doc/discord-ducking/

MSG
}

post_upgrade() {
  cat <<'MSG'

  discord-ducking upgraded. To apply changed templates to your user config:
      discord-ducking install
  Your compressor threshold is preserved.

MSG
}

pre_remove() {
  cat <<'MSG'

  Note: per-user config in ~/.config is NOT removed by pacman.
  Run this first if you have not already:
      discord-ducking uninstall

MSG
}
